1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,840
Reading Task

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:04,680
The Oxford English Dictionary

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:09,900
You may think that English dictionaries have been used for many, many centuries.

4
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:16,140
The spelling of English has always been a problem but it was more of a problem in the days

5
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:17,340
before a dictionary.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,540
Then people could spell words in different ways which you might find interesting.

7
00:00:22,580 --> 00:00:26,140
But it made reading English much more difficult.

8
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:32,340
So dictionaries were invented to encourage everybody to spell the same.

9
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:38,040
In fact, an English dictionary like the kind you use today wasn’t made

10
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:40,380
until the time of the late Qing Dynasty.

11
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:44,880
Three men did most of the important early work on dictionaries:

12
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:49,060
Samuel Johnson, Noah Webster, and James Murray.

13
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:53,580
These men spent nearly all of their lives trying to collect words

14
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:54,540
for their dictionaries.

15
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:57,540
For them, it wasn’t only a job;

16
00:00:57,860 --> 00:01:00,160
it was a wonderful journey of discovery.

17
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:04,560
The largest dictionary in the world is the Oxford English Dictionary,

18
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:07,480
or OED for short.

19
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:13,600
The idea for this dictionary came from an important meeting in Britain in 1857.

20
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:21,140
Twenty-two years later, Oxford University asked James Murray to be the editor of its new dictionary.

21
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:23,640
Murray had never been to college.

22
00:01:23,640 --> 00:01:28,000
At the age of fourteen, he left his village school in Scotland

23
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,240
and taught himself while working in a bank.

24
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:33,880
Later he became a great teacher.

25
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,660
After Oxford gave him the job,

26
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:41,220
Murray had a place built in the garden behind his house to do his work.

27
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:44,020
Part of it was one metre underground.

28
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:49,180
In winter it felt like a barn, he had to wear a heavy coat

29
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:51,800
and put his feet in a box to keep warm.

30
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,140
Murray hoped to finish the new dictionary in ten years.

31
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:10,600
But after five years, he was still adding words for the letter A!

32
00:02:10,980 --> 00:02:12,880
Then others went to work with Murray,

33
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:14,780
including his two daughters.

34
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:18,560
He worked on the dictionary until he was very old.

35
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:22,300
Forty-four years later, in 1928,

36
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:24,680
other editors finished it.

37
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:32,620
And you thought your English dictionary was big!
